BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Gift in Motion — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261107T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261107T170000
DTSTAMP:20261108T221058Z
UID:04f24907bb0bccb4d5e9df7d18ee@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:A multidisciplinary showcase from the artists and scholars of 
 the full-time faculty and staff at the Corcoran School of the Arts & Desig
 n. A Gift In Motion loosely draws from Robin Wall Kimmerer’s book The Se
 rviceberry which uplifts Indigenous frameworks of the natural world to adv
 ance the possibilities in collectivity\, reciprocity\, and the mindset of 
 abundance. These are anti-capitalist models and theories that provide insi
 ght into the systems of power we negotiate\, but also offer a lens on how 
 we individually move through our daily lives—together—through historic
 ally fraught landscapes. A keen reminder that working with\, for\, and bec
 ause of each other remains the most human motivation\, eve\nCategory: Arts
  & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/a-gift-in-mot
 ion
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CANCELLED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/a-gift-in-motion
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Brady Art Gallery Tour: Unfossilizing African Art and Cracks & Ref
 orms — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261107T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261107T140000
DTSTAMP:20261108T221058Z
UID:b1e0933765e4ebf26d1ce0242d90@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Take a student-led tour of the exhibitions currently on view a
 t the Brady Art Gallery: "Unfossilizing African Art" and "Cracks and Refor
 ms". Unfossilizing African Art recontextualizes African material culture o
 bjects that have been held in the GW Collection for nearly 50 years. Led b
 y Assistant Professor Clement Akpang\, 59 students across four seminar cou
 rses conducted original research to deepen understanding of the collection
  and reconsider how these objects are interpreted and exhibited. Cracks & 
 Reforms: The 1920s Through the GW Collection examines a decade shaped by p
 olitical upheaval\, social transformation\, migration\, modernization\, an
 d cultural redefinition. This exhibition\, developed\nCategory: Arts & Cul
 ture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/brady-art-gallery-t
 our-unfossilizing-african-art-and-cracks-reforms
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CANCELLED
